      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Insecticides.",
              "pdf_page": 1252,
              "printed_page": 1170,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pyrethrins—natural.",
              "pdf_page": 1252,
              "printed_page": 1170,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pyrethroids—synthetic.",
              "pdf_page": 1252,
              "printed_page": 1170,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Based on cases managed by the ASPCA Animal Poison Control Center exposures reported by generic from most to least are permethrin, deltamethrin, flumethrin, etofenprox, pyrethrins, cyfluthrin, cyphenothrin,",
              "pdf_page": 1252,
              "printed_page": 1170,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "imiprothrin and lambda cyhalothrin.",
              "pdf_page": 1252,
              "printed_page": 1170,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Affect the nervous system—reversibly prolong sodium conductance in nerve axons, resulting in",
              "pdf_page": 1252,
              "printed_page": 1170,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "repetitive nerve discharges.",
              "pdf_page": 1252,
              "printed_page": 1170,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "With the advent of oral insecticides, safer topicals, and improved client education pyrethrin/pyrethroid adverse event reports continue to decrease. In 2017, 15.1% of all insecticide-related calls to the ASPCA Animal Poison Control Center were attributed to pyrethrin/pyrethroid-containing products.",
              "pdf_page": 1252,
              "printed_page": 1170,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
